  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A tank of nuclear waste exploded and nobody was told. The southern Urals, September 1957.",
        "visual_query": "ural industrial landscape winter",
        "visual_fallback": "ural hills"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Mayak made plutonium for Soviet bombs. One waste tank lost its cooling, baked itself dry, and went off with the force of seventy tonnes of TNT.",
        "visual_query": "soviet era industrial plant",
        "visual_fallback": "soviet factory"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The blast threw a radioactive plume hundreds of kilometres downwind. Villagers kept working their fields for a week before anyone came to move them. How do you hide something that size?",
        "visual_query": "russian village countryside fields",
        "visual_fallback": "rural fields"
      },
      {
        "narration": "You leave the town off the map. Chelyabinsk-40 was printed nowhere, so the accident got named after Kyshtym, the nearest place that officially existed.",
        "visual_query": "cold war soviet archive",
        "visual_fallback": "old archive"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Twenty million curies. Ten thousand people moved. No admission until 1989. It is still the only accident anywhere rated level six.",
        "visual_query": "abandoned soviet village ruins",
        "visual_fallback": "abandoned village"
      }
    ],
