        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "Mayak was a plutonium reprocessing plant for Soviet nuclear weapons; a cooling system failure let the waste dry to ammonium nitrate and acetate residue, which exploded with a force of at least 70 tons of TNT.",
        "segment_index": 1,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "83% of the claim's wording appears in the reference article, including 1 number(s)",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "About 2 million curies went into a plume spread over hundreds of kilometres, and evacuations only began on 6 October 1957, a week after the explosion.",
        "segment_index": 2,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "82% of the claim's wording appears in the reference article, including 3 number(s)",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "The plant stood in the closed city of Chelyabinsk-40, which was not marked on maps, so the accident was named after Kyshtym, the nearest town that was.",
        "segment_index": 3,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "94% of the claim's wording appears in the reference article, including 1 number(s)",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "An estimated 20 million curies was released in total, about 10,000 people were evacuated, the Soviet government only began declassifying the documents in 1989, and it remains the only accident rated INES level 6.",
